  "problem": "If ${\\int }_{-10}^{-7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi -15}{2},$ then ${\\int }_{-10}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "From part $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{15+9\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nand from the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-10}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-10}^{-7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-7}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-10}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{\\pi -15}{2}+\\frac{15+9\\pi }{2}=5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
